---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Joe Saia"
  description="Engineering and data leader. PhD economist, ex-Federal Reserve. 15 years building software, data, and the teams behind them in healthtech and startups."
>
  <section class="space-y-10">
    <div class="space-y-4">
      <h1
        class="text-4xl font-bold tracking-tight text-text-heading sm:text-5xl"
      >
        Joe Saia
      </h1>
      <p class="text-sm font-medium uppercase tracking-[0.2em] text-accent">
        Engineering and data leadership · Federal Reserve to PhD economist to
        Seed/Series A/Growth Stage startups
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="space-y-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-text-secondary">
      <p>
        Hi, I'm Joe Saia. For 15 years I've built software, data systems, and
        the teams that ship them — sometimes as a senior engineer, sometimes as
        the person leading the team. That includes an economics PhD at Columbia,
        research at the Federal Reserve Board of Governors, and work across
        seed, Series A, and growth-stage companies, most recently in healthtech.
        If you want to know why we should work together, the four points below
        are a start. If you'd rather see what I've been thinking, the <a
          href="/writing/"
          class="underline decoration-border underline-offset-4 transition-colors hover:text-text-heading hover:decoration-border-hover"
          >blog is here</a
        >.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="space-y-8 text-lg leading-relaxed text-text-secondary">
      <p>
        First, I build high-performance teams both as a leader and as a member.
        A good team creates leverage for every member and ends up more than the
        sum of its parts; creating that is deliberate work: hiring, ways of
        working, and the trust that lets people argue and ship anyway. I've led
        a 25-person engineering and data org across two continents, worked
        inside a global four-person team punching far above its weight, and have
        been a member of a corporate Senior Leadership Team. I think about each
        of those shapes seriously.
      </p>
      <p>
        Second, I want to be part of building something impactful that improves
        the world. I've worked as a public servant in the Federal Reserve,
        contributing to monetary policy that drives the world's largest economy,
        and in healthtech, building the technology that improves individual
        lives.
      </p>
      <p>
        Third, I want colleagues who push me. I've gone deep on theory — a
        Columbia PhD in economics, Bayesian and causal-inference research at the
        Federal Reserve — and wide on practice, building data infrastructure, ML
        pipelines, and engineering teams across seed, Series A, and growth-stage
        companies. I'm looking for peers who bring their own depth-and-breadth
        combinations.
      </p>
      <p>
        Fourth, I'm pragmatic about AI. I've built AI tools embedded in
        production applications such as triage assistants, and I think we're at
        the start of a shift comparable to the second Industrial Revolution. I'm
        also realistic that today's models have real limits, and that we haven't
        yet figured out how to reshape work around them. I am excited to work
        that out: what to automate, what to keep human, and how teams change
        shape.
      </p>
      <p>
        Those are the four points. If they describe a team you're building — or
        one you'd want to put me in touch with — <a
          href="/contact/"
          class="underline decoration-border underline-offset-4 transition-colors hover:text-text-heading hover:decoration-border-hover"
          >let's talk</a
        >.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="flex flex-wrap gap-4 pt-2">
      <a
        href="/writing/"
        class="inline-flex items-center rounded bg-btn-bg px-4 py-2 text-sm font-medium text-text-heading transition-colors hover:bg-btn-bg-hover"
      >
        Read the Blog
      </a>
      <a
        href="/contact/"
        class="inline-flex items-center rounded border border-border px-4 py-2 text-sm font-medium transition-colors hover:border-border-hover"
      >
        Get in Touch
      </a>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
